                                                              "imageUrl":  "https://blogger.googleusercontent.com/img/b/R29vZ2xl/AVvXsEgh5fXUk3rKQgi_I28N1acK-5yXvwUPErlVboSe6pyCHZL8x0-r5qMMHlHdVhSxhBxFeuLGARgSFuwvCTDO_AdIgPsSjnpLeGDHHWmsXuT9hy7GID8Rkfe5QL_54HQTfOG1JUheL-vNqI2rSUlHl79wOqBiuwCGhvwRaQkpP2pQrPQ_PantALROtViRqHw/s1178/Khoslatweet.jpg",
                                                              "summary":  "Damodaran argues that VC\u0027s incentive structure ÃƒÂ¢Ã¢â€šÂ¬Ã¢â‚¬Â built around power-law returns ÃƒÂ¢Ã¢â€šÂ¬Ã¢â‚¬Â systematically pushes founders toward maximum scaling at the expense of profitability, even when a profitable niche strategy would create more durable value. He traces how changes in capital markets, including crossover funds investing in late-stage private companies and weakened valuation discipline in public markets, have amplified this bias. The result is that today\u0027s IPO cohort is larger in revenue but far less profitable than historical predecessors. He warns this disconnect between scale and value creation is a structural flaw, not a temporary growing pain.",
                                                              "take":  "This is a sharp structural critique with direct implications for late-stage private and public tech investing right now. Damodaran is essentially saying the VC model has infected public markets with a growth-at-all-costs ethos that doesn\u0027t survive contact with real cost-of-capital discipline. For public market investors, the takeaway is to be skeptical of high-revenue, low-margin IPOs that can\u0027t articulate a clear path to profitability at current scale. The AI investment wave is particularly susceptible to this exact flaw: massive infrastructure spend, impressive revenue, but unit economics that only pencil out if the market grows 10x. That\u0027s a bet, not a business model.",
