                                                              "summary":  "Brown argues that current skepticism about Big Tech capex is rooted in underestimating the scale of future AI infrastructure demand. Drawing on insights from tech executives, he contends that the coming wave of autonomous AI agents ÃƒÂ¢Ã¢â€šÂ¬Ã¢â‚¬Â serving potentially a billion users ÃƒÂ¢Ã¢â€šÂ¬Ã¢â‚¬Â will require vastly more compute than today\u0027s chatbots and coding tools consume. He points to autonomous vehicles, humanoid robots, and automated manufacturing as demand vectors that will dwarf current usage. The piece frames current tech stock volatility as a mispricing born of anchoring to today\u0027s use cases rather than the near-future agentic paradigm.",
                                                              "take":  "Brown is making a bull case for sustained AI infrastructure investment that cuts against the \u0027capex bubble\u0027 narrative gaining traction in 2026. The key distinction he\u0027s drawing ÃƒÂ¢Ã¢â€šÂ¬Ã¢â‚¬Â between current-use compute and future-agent compute ÃƒÂ¢Ã¢â€šÂ¬Ã¢â‚¬Â is legitimate and underappreciated. If agentic AI scales to mass deployment, the compute multiplier per user is orders of magnitude higher than today\u0027s inference-heavy workloads. That said, the argument also functions as a convenient rationalization for incumbents to keep spending; the risk is that the demand timeline slips while interest expense and depreciation compound today. Investors should weight this bullishly for hyperscalers with diversified revenue (AWS, Azure, Google Cloud) and more cautiously for pure-play AI infrastructure names where the whole thesis depends on the demand wave arriving on schedule.",
