                                                              "summary":  "Brown profiles Ron Baron and his son Michael, tracing how Baron Capital grew from modest origins to managing roughly $70 billion while returning over $60 billion to investors since the 1980s ÃƒÂ¢Ã¢â€šÂ¬Ã¢â‚¬Â the best-performing major mutual fund over that span. The outperformance is largely attributable to early, concentrated positions in Tesla (purchased at $14/share in 2014) and SpaceX (now a $25B position), held through severe drawdowns on the conviction that visionary founders compound value over long horizons. The piece draws a contrast between Baron\u0027s willingness to make concentrated bets and the industry constraints that prevented most peers from replicating those returns.",
                                                              "take":  "The Baron story is instructive but requires careful unpacking before drawing investing lessons. The $60 billion return is real, but it is heavily concentrated in two Musk-led bets ÃƒÂ¢Ã¢â€šÂ¬Ã¢â‚¬Â Tesla and SpaceX ÃƒÂ¢Ã¢â€šÂ¬Ã¢â‚¬Â which means attribution risk is enormous. The lesson most readers will take is \u0027concentrate in visionary founders,\u0027 but the lesson that deserves equal weight is survivorship bias: for every Ron Baron/Tesla pairing, there are dozens of concentrated conviction bets on visionary founders that went to zero. The genuinely replicable part of Baron\u0027s edge is the long holding period and the tolerance for drawdowns ÃƒÂ¢Ã¢â€šÂ¬Ã¢â‚¬Â behaviors retail investors claim to have but consistently abandon. If you take one thing from this piece, it is that time horizon is the actual alpha source, and most investors trade it away for comfort.",
